{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:InformationTheory.QHeatKernelPeterWeyl","@type":"LeanModule","name":"InformationTheory.QHeatKernelPeterWeyl","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Combinatorics.Young.YoungDiagram","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Int.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Exp","lean:module:Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.InfiniteSum.Basic"],"declarations":11,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.qHeatKernelPeterWeyl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.qHeatKernelPeterWeyl","kind":"def","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.lean#L146-L167","doc":"**Peter-Weyl heat-kernel convergence** (conjecture, archimedean).\n\nThe named statement of `prop:q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl`: for `0 < q`,\n`q ≠ 1` and a heat time `t > 0`, the spectral series\n\n  `Σ_{λ} d_λ(q) · exp(-μ_λ(q) · t)`\n\nof the `q`-heat kernel is (absolutely) summable over the Wedderburn\nblocks `λ` (indexed by `YoungDiagram`), where `μ_λ(q) = muLambda q λ`\nis the content-Casimir eigenvalue and `dLambda λ` is the Steinberg\n`q`-hook dimension of the block.\n\nThis is the Mercer-type convergence whose justification (`μ_λ ≍ n²/q²`\nfor `λ ⊢ n` against polynomial-in-`n` dimensions) is given in the\nnarrative but not yet formalised; per AGENTS.md §1a / §3b (Pattern C)\nwe **name** the conditional conjecture as a `Prop` rather than fake a\nproof. The carrier asserts the real `Summable` claim — there is no\ninstance / proof term, the honest open state. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl","label":"prop:q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl","md":"quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.muLambda_from_sq_bracket","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.muLambda_from_sq_bracket","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.lean#L116-L144","doc":"**μ_λ decomposition via Jackson shift**: the content-Casimir formula\n`μ_λ(q) = ℏ_q² · Σ [c]_q[c+1]_q` decomposes via `jacksonInt_mul_succ` as\n\n  `μ_λ(q) = ℏ_q² · (q · Σ [c]_q² + Σ [c]_q)`\n\nThe `q · Σ [c]_q²` term connects to the JM cosh eigenvalue via\n`cosh_eq_sq_bracket_add_two` (in `QJucysMurphy.lean`):\n\n  `Σ_{□∈λ} (q^{2c} + q^{-2c}) = Σ_{□∈λ} ((q^c - q^{-c})² + 2)`\n\nNote: `(q^c - q^{-c})` is NOT `(q - q⁻¹)·[c]_q` in general — the\ncosh-to-bracket bridge goes through `(q^c - q^{-c})²`, not `[c]_q²`.\nThe two are related by `(q^c - q^{-c})² = (q - q⁻¹)²·[c]_{q²}²` where\n`[c]_{q²}` is the `q²`-integer, not the `q`-integer.  The decomposition\nhere uses `[c]_q` (the `q`-integer from `contentSumQCasimir`) directly.\n\nBoth `Σ [c]_q²` and `Σ [c]_q` are determined by the shape λ;\nno free parameter remains. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.jacksonInt_mul_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.jacksonInt_mul_succ","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.lean#L93-L114","doc":"**Jackson-integer shift identity**: `[k]_q · [k+1]_q = q · [k]_q² + [k]_q`.\n\nThe q-deformation of `k(k+1) = k² + k`.  Uses the Jackson recurrence\n`[k+1]_q = 1 + q·[k]_q`, which gives\n`[k]_q · [k+1]_q = [k]_q · (1 + q·[k]_q) = [k]_q + q·[k]_q²`.\n\nAt `q = 1`, the extra factor `q` disappears and this reduces to `k(k+1) = k² + k`.\nThe q-weighted form is the correct bridge between `contentSumQCasimir`\n(which sums `[c]_q[c+1]_q`) and the squared-bracket form `[c]_q²`\nfrom the bar-symmetrised JM cosh sum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.muLambda_single","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.muLambda_single","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.lean#L86-L91","doc":"**Single-cell partition** λ = (1) has μ_λ = 0. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.singleCellDiagram","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.singleCellDiagram","kind":"def","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.lean#L73-L84","doc":"The single-cell Young diagram λ = (1), i.e. cells `{(0,0)}`. The\nsingleton is a lower set because `(0,0)` is the minimum of `ℕ × ℕ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.muLambda_classical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.muLambda_classical","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.lean#L65-L71","doc":"**At q = 1** (classical limit): μ_λ reduces to the classical\ncontent sum (the spin term vanishes because ℏ_q = 0). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.muLambda","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.muLambda","kind":"def","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.lean#L55-L63","doc":"**Reeb-spin eigenvalue on Wedderburn block**:\n\nμ_λ(q) = ℏ_q² · Σ_{□ ∈ λ} [c(□)]_q · [c(□)+1]_q.\n\nThe base-manifold eigenvalue q⁻² |k|² is separate and depends\non the spatial mode, not on the partition λ. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.contentSumQCasimir","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.contentSumQCasimir","kind":"def","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.lean#L49-L53","doc":"q-Casimir content-sum for a partition: Σ [c]_q [c+1]_q. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.contentSumClassical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.contentSumClassical","kind":"def","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.lean#L44-L47","doc":"Classical content-sum for a partition: Σ c(c+1). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.hbarQ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.hbarQ","kind":"def","line":40,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.lean#L40-L42","doc":"Quantum Planck constant ℏ_q = (q - 1)/q. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.jacksonInt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.jacksonInt","kind":"def","line":34,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/q-heat-kernel-peter-weyl.lean#L34-L38","doc":"Jackson integer [k]_q = (1 - q^k)/(1 - q) over ℝ (q ≠ 1). ","signatureChunk":0}]}